  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nToday through Tonight\nA welcome break from the recent heat wave continues the rest of\ntoday under the influence of a surface ridge centered over the\nGreat Lakes. This high is maintaining a modest easterly breeze \nacross central Illinois, drawing in a much drier airmass with \ndewpoints in the upper 50s to lower 60s. High temperatures this \nafternoon will top out in the low to mid 80s under mostly sunny \nskies, followed by a quiet and comfortable night with lows in \nthe lower 60s.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday (Return of Summer Heat)\nThe respite from the heat will be short-lived as summer-like \nconditions return. An upper-level low currently over the Pacific\nNorthwest is projected to track eastward into the Canadian \nPrairies by Tuesday, flattening the strong central U.S. upper \nridge and pushing its warm eastern periphery back across the \nMidwest. An associated 850mb thermal ridge (with temperatures \nclimbing to 22-24C, reminiscent of the recent heat wave) will \nshift over central Illinois Tuesday, then progress into southern\nIllinois by Wednesday. Highs will surge back into the 90s \nacross most of central Illinois on Tuesday.\n\nFortunately, southerly flow will not be established long enough\nby Tuesday afternoon for notable moisture return; deep boundary\nlayer mixing will keep dewpoints relatively low (low to mid \n60s), holding heat indices in check in the low to mid 90s. \n\nMoisture returns in earnest on Wednesday as southwesterly flow \nstrengthens ahead of an approaching cold front. This flow will \ntransport a much muggier airmass into the region, boosting \ndewpoints into the low to mid 70s. Combined with temperatures in\nthe low to mid 90s, heat indices are expected to exceed 100 \ndegrees in several locations Wednesday afternoon, particularly \nsouth of the I-72 corridor.\n\nWednesday through Thursday (Cold Front & Storm Potential)\nThe cold front is modeled to cross central Illinois mid-to- \nlate Wednesday morning. Due to the timing near the diurnal \ninstability minimum, instability will be weak (GFS MLCAPE values\nat or below 500 J/kg), and initial convection is expected to be\nin a decaying phase as it enters the area. However, the frontal\nboundary is projected to slow and stall as it moves south \nacross the I-72 and I-70 corridors Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night. A second round of convection is expected to \ndevelop along a modest low-level jet to our west and track \neastward along the stalling boundary overnight. Total rainfall \nthrough Thursday is forecast to average 0.25 to 0.50 inches \nnorth of I-72/Danville, and 0.50 to 0.75 inches to the south. \nThe 90th percentile guidance suggests localized training of \nstorms could produce isolated streaks of 1.0 to 1.5 inches.\n\nFriday through Sunday\nHigh pressure will build across the Upper Midwest behind the \nfront on Thursday, gradually shifting east across the Great \nLakes by Friday. While dry weather will prevail further north \nunder the influence of this ridge, some precipitation chances \nwill linger across central and southern Illinois in the vicinity\nof the stalled frontal boundary. The upper-level pattern is \nmodeled to flatten further over the weekend, with low-amplitude \nwaves in the zonal flow bringing periodic chances for showers \nand storms. Due to low predictability and model spread, broad- \nbrushed 20-30% PoPs remain in place from Friday through early \nnext week.",
